        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "For fixed genetic variance  $ G_{yy} $, Eq. (24) shows that the time required for genetic assimilation is proportional to the sum of the inverses of the selection pressure  $ (s) $ and the plasticity cost  $ (c) $. Under mutation–selection balance, Eq. (25) instead shows that the relaxation time scales with the square root of this sum. In both cases, if either the environmental selection pressure is absent  $ (s \\to 0) $ or the plasticity is cost-free  $ (c \\to 0) $, the relaxation time diverges  $ (\\tau \\to \\infty) $. While phenotypic plasticity allows organisms to survive sudden environmental changes, the cost of maintaining this plasticity drives the subsequent genetic assimilation.",
            "block_bbox": [
                101,
                674,
                601,
                953
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 8,
            "group_id": 8,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    101.0,
                    674.0
                ],
                [
                    601.0,
                    674.0
                ],
                [
                    601.0,
                    953.0
